`method-call-recovery-linker` emits 64 edges on a single-backend Python
self-survey, of which 16 are wrong. Two of them bound a `calls` edge to a
member that cannot be called: `m.start()` / `m.end()` on a `re.Match` became
`ItemIdMatch.start` / `ItemIdMatch.end`, both declared `int`. Calling an int
instance is a TypeError, so the binding sits outside this linker's own premise
-- it stamps `call_construct="method"`.

THE GATE READS THE DECLARED TYPE, NOT THE SYMBOL KIND. A blanket
`kind == "field"` refusal would be wrong: a JS/TS class property and a Python
dataclass field can both hold a callable, and `js_ts.py` records
`property_signature` members as `field`. The refusal is positive -- it fires
only when a declaration is PRESENT and every arm of it is a builtin scalar or
container. Refused: `int`, `list[str]`, `Optional[str]`, `str | None`. Not
refused: `Callable[[], None]`, a project class, an absent declaration, and any
wrapper the parser has not been taught (`ClassVar[...]`, `Union[...]`).
Under-refusal is the safe direction: a missed refusal leaves one wrong edge,
an over-eager one silently deletes a correct recovery.

A PARENTHESIS DISCRIMINATES A VALUE TYPE FROM A CALL SIGNATURE. `Symbol.signature`
holds `int` for a field and `(self) -> int` for a method; reading the second as
the first would refuse real methods, so a parenthesised signature is declined
rather than parsed. That also keeps the callable-KINDS question out of it --
`ranking._CALLABLE_KINDS` and `entrypoints._CALLABLE_KINDS` differ deliberately
and have cross-referencing comments saying so since 28d26c92c8.

THE REFUSAL DROPS A CANDIDATE, NOT THE CALL SITE, so a sibling class that really
does declare the method still wins. Pinned by a test where the non-callable's
hint sits NEARER the call and would otherwise win the line-proximity tiebreaker.

